in this episode of cloud performance  atlas I helped some IOT visionaries come  to terms with load balancer performance  well their bet on the future of  technology pay off stay tuned to find  out  IOT roulette is a company which offers a  scalable way for IOT devices to connect  with cloud backends through a common and  simple SDK and transport layer a few  weeks ago I met with their CEO to talk  about the biggest thing on her mind  what's the performance overhead of using  Jeep's EPS load balancer as you can  imagine scale is really important to IOT  roulette with an estimated 50 billion  IOT devices in the world by 2020 scaling  your services to handle the sudden load  of a million new devices is an important  feature so fast performing load  balancers are crucial to get this right  but before we start talking about the  balancers we need to see how things  perform directly to get a baseline  performance we set up an instance in US  central and kicked off a bunch of curl  commands from a machine in Europe now if  you do this test yourself you'll notice  that the curl performance can end up  being double what the ping time is  simply due to the overhead of TCP  handshakes involved in the transfer of  the fetched data now Google cloud  platform provides two primary load  balancers so we need to understand the  difference and profile each one  individually the first is a TCP UDP load  balancer which acts much like you'd  expect a request comes in from the  client to the load balancer and is  forwarded along to the backend directly  the HTTP load balancer on the other hand  has some serious magic going on it  proxies traffic through Google front end  servers first which stops the session on  the public Internet and continues on  using Google's private network so  depending on which balance you're using  we should expect different set of  performance characteristics to test this  we set up instance groups for each  balancer in the US central region and  had a load generator in Europe fetch  from them using the same curl commands  as before the results show some  interesting characteristics first the  TCP balancer shows very similar results  to the direct access version this is  somewhat expected in this case traffic  moves from the user to the load  balancers which are in the region where  the VMS are located as such performance  is a factor of the distance from the  requester to the closest entry in the  target region just like directly  accessing the VM  hdtb balance or on the other hand did  something I really wasn't expecting  according to this test it appears to  help or mow the direct connection and  the TCP balancer this is odd since the  HTTP load balancer effectively adds  another hop to each fetch instead of  going from the clients to the balancer  to the backend it has the intermediate  step of hitting the front-end boxes  first now at first glance this seems  confusing about why adding an extra hop  would improve performance but once we  look under the hood we can quickly see  why this works see when a request hits  the HTTP bouncer the TCP session stops  there the GSEs then move the request  onto the private network and all further  interactions happen between the GFE and  your specific back-end now here's the  important part  after a GFE connects to a back-end to  handle the request it keeps that  connection open this means that future  requests from this GFE to this specific  back-end will not need the overhead of  committed creating a connection and  instead can actually just go to sending  data ASAP the result of this setup means  that the first query that causes the GFE  to open a connection to the back-end  will see slightly higher response times  those are the spikes in the front of the  graph however subsequent packets rather  the same back-end will see a lower  minimum latency so for IOT roulette this  is fantastic  the more IOT devices they get in a  region the better their HTTP load  balancers will perform not a bad bet to  make if you'd like more details on this  problem check out the full article  linked in the description below and make  sure to subscribe to our google cloud  youtube channel to get alerts when new  performance videos comes out because  don't forget when it comes to  performance every millisecond counts
 

